#!/usr/bin/env python3.11
"""pr-state-capture — per-repository pull-request state, captured as signals.

Deck's PR-ladder panel needs a per-product pull-request inventory (number,
title, author, draft flag, head sha, state), including recently-closed PRs, and
its done-when requires the derivation spawn no `gh` process in Deck
(issue_01M0NWVW4YS8E04A657JD04QQD). This reads that inventory and emits one
signal per (repo, pr, head sha, state, draft flag, title) transition.

AUTHENTICATES AS THE INSTALL'S OWN GITHUB APP (`github_app.installed_app()`), never
`gh`. `gh` on this machine rides the operator's personal token, which carries
`repo` and `workflow` WRITE scopes org-wide — reading PR state for a dashboard
does not need that reach, and platform/decisions/0051 already commits to every
`gh` call moving off it. The Bosun's own gates (draft/mergeability/CI checks at
merge time) still use `gh` today; this script does not touch that path.

WHY THIS IS A SEPARATE READ FROM THE GATES' LIVE GITHUB READ, AND MUST STAY
SEPARATE: a merge gate needs GitHub's answer AT THE MOMENT it decides, because
a stale answer there admits or blocks the wrong PR. A dashboard panel is fine
lagging one capture interval — nobody is blocked on it being current to the
second. Collapsing the two into one source would either slow the gate down to
a capture cadence or speed the capture up to gate cost for no reader who needs
it; a later change that "consolidates" them for being 'the same read' would be
making exactly that mistake. See `lib/agent-platform/github_app.py`'s docstring
for the shared authentication code both paths could in principle use — sharing
the JWT/token plumbing is fine; sharing the READ is not.

READ-ONLY. The install's own App holds `pull_requests:write` and
`contents:write` for other reasons (its own merge/gate duties elsewhere); this
script mints its OWN installation tokens, one per GitHub owner it reads
(see "ONE INSTALLATION PER OWNER" below), each scoped to `pull_requests: read`
and `metadata: read` (`_READ_ONLY_PERMISSIONS`), so a bug here that tried to
write would be refused by GitHub itself, not merely by review. The only
non-GET HTTP calls anywhere in this file are the installation-token mints
(`github_app.installation_token`, a POST to `/access_tokens`, one per owner)
— a GitHub App authentication handshake, not a write to any repository's
content. Nothing here issues a POST, PATCH, PUT or DELETE against a
`/repos/...` endpoint.

ONE INSTALLATION PER OWNER, NOT ONE FOR THE WHOLE RUN. `repos_to_capture()`
returns `owner/repo` for every registered repo via
`projects.repo_github_slug`, and most of those repos are `convivy/...` — but
not all: Shipwright's `homebrew-tap` is registered under the `convivybuilder`
GitHub owner (the project registry's `repo_owners`), its own GitHub App
installation, separate from `convivy`'s. `main()` groups the repos by owner
and resolves each owner's installation and token independently, so a
failure resolving one owner costs only that owner's repos rather than the
whole pass.

AN OWNER THE APP IS NOT INSTALLED ON IS SKIPPED, NOT FAILED (operator's
decision, 2026-10-15). `GA.installation_for_owner` raises `GA.NotInstalled`
— never a plain `GA.Unreadable` — only when BOTH its lookups (the org
endpoint, then the user endpoint) answer 404: GitHub's own confirmation the
App has no installation there, not merely a read that failed. `main()`
catches `GA.NotInstalled` ahead of `GA.Unreadable`, prints one notice naming
the owner and its registered-repo count, and moves on without setting
`exit_code` — the same "skip only what's confirmed absent" posture
`installed_repos`'s docstring already describes for one repo within an
installed owner. The project registry registers three projects under a
personal GitHub account that the App has no installation on; since the
owner-grouping change deployed on 2026-10-15 (launchd run 464 onward),
every pass exited 1 on exactly that owner, every time — the same shape of
incident the 2026-09-16 entry above describes for five unpushed repos, a
permanent failure hiding a real one. If the operator wants that account's
pull requests captured, the operator moves those repos to an owner the
App is installed on; until then this pass leaves them uncaptured rather
than failing on them every time. Any OTHER failure resolving an
owner — a non-404 on either lookup, a missing installation id, a token
this run cannot mint — is still `GA.Unreadable`, is not caught by the
`NotInstalled` branch, and still fails the pass exactly as before: that is
a failed read, not a confirmed absence, and must stay loud.

THE SIGNAL'S OWN IDENTITY IS STILL THE CORRECTNESS BACKSTOP; A LOCAL
SEND-STATE RECORD IS WHAT KEEPS A PASS FROM RE-POSTING EVERYTHING
(issue_01M3WQQ1STSGNZ6SKSMFGSBS4X). Each signal's `dedup_key` is still built
from (repo, pr number, head sha, state, draft flag, title hash) —
`dedup_key_for` — and `signal_client.post_structured` still passes it
straight through to the store, which still treats a repeated `dedup_key` as
a no-op duplicate (the same mechanism `agentctl`'s per-run model-resolution
record and `post-changelog`'s idempotent re-post both use); that is why a
missing or wiped local record is safe to fall back to "emit everything" from
— the store's own dedup still collapses anything it has already seen back
down to a no-op.

This script used to rely on that alone: every pass re-listed every covered
repo's PRs in full (`state=all`) and re-emitted every row, leaving the store
to turn an unchanged row into "no second row". At this platform's scale
that meant posting thousands of mostly-unchanged rows every 900 seconds
against the Library's 120-signals-a-minute-per-submitter ceiling
(library#176) — see `_RUN_DEADLINE_SECONDS` for what that did to a real run.
`load_delivered`/`save_delivered` now keep, per repo under `STATE_DIR`, the
last identity (`head_sha`, `state`, `draft`, `title_hash`) actually
delivered for each PR number, and a pass emits a row only when the
freshly-read identity differs from that record (`_changed_rows`). Losing
that record (missing, corrupt, a wiped `STATE_DIR`) means "nothing
delivered yet" for the affected repo and the next pass emits every row for
it — the same self-heals-on-a-missed-run property the old all-rows design
had; what changed is that the common case (nothing changed) no longer costs
a POST at all.

THE KEY'S TITLE COMPONENT IS A HASH OF THE FULL TITLE'S IDENTITY FORM, NOT THE
200-CHAR DISPLAY VALUE (security-reviewer, #595 round 3). `row["title"]` is
capped at 200 characters for display, so two GitHub titles differing only
after character 200 would sanitise to the same displayed string — keying
dedup on that value would collapse them into one row and silently drop the
second title's transition. `rows_from_pulls` hashes the uncapped
`_identity_title(raw_title)` into `row["title_hash"]`
(`hashlib.sha256(...).hexdigest()[:16]`, the same short-hash convention
`agentctl` and `session_view.py` already use for a composite key); a hash
rather than the raw text because GitHub-authored free text has no length bound
this script controls, and a dedup key is not the place to carry an unbounded
string.

DISPLAY AND IDENTITY ARE TWO SEPARATE DERIVATIONS OF THE SAME RAW TITLE
(security-reviewer, #595 round 4; split further in round 5), because they
answer different questions. `row["title"]` (display) is
`SC.sanitise(raw_title, max_len=200)`: NFC-normalised, ANSI/control
characters stripped, credentials redacted, and only the DISPLAY-safe subset
of Default_Ignorable Unicode codepoints stripped (see `signal_client.
_DISPLAY_KEEP_CODEPOINTS`) — round 4 stripped the full property here and
broke real rendering (a ZWJ-joined family emoji split into four separate
people; a gitmoji recycle symbol lost its forced emoji presentation), so
round 5 narrowed this to codepoints whose removal cannot change what a
reader sees. `title_hash` (identity) additionally NFKC-normalises
`SC.sanitise`'s output — see `_identity_title` below — because two titles a
human reads as the SAME text must hash the same, and NFC alone leaves
compatibility-equivalent forms distinct (a fullwidth "Ａ" from an ASCII "A",
the "ﬁ" ligature from "fi", a circled "①" from "1" — round-3's own
non-blocking finding); NFKC is not applied for display, since it changes
what the text actually SAYS. Identity then removes every REMAINING
Default_Ignorable codepoint via `signal_client.is_default_ignorable`
directly, with no keep list — including the ones display now keeps (ZWJ,
the variation selectors, ZWNJ, soft hyphen) — because identity's contract is
narrower than display's: two titles a reader cannot visually distinguish
must hash the same, and none of those four codepoints changes what a title
SAYS the way NFKC's compatibility folding would if applied to display. Both
extra folds live here, in this script's own identity derivation, rather than
in `signal_client.sanitise`, where every other caller displays its output
unmodified (see that module's docstring for the full caller audit).

The draft and title fields are in the key too, not just the row, because each
can move on its own with no new commit and no state transition — a
draft-to-ready flip and a title edit both leave `head_sha` and `state`
unchanged. Leaving either out of the key would make that change invisible:
the dedup key would repeat, the store would call it a duplicate, and Deck
would keep rendering the stale badge or the stale title until some unrelated
transition (a push, a close) happened to fire a new row. This widens the
signal's grain from "the PR's (sha, state) changed" to "any field Deck's
panel renders changed" — deliberately, since a stale render is the failure
this capture exists to prevent. `author` and `number` are not in the key:
GitHub does not let either change after a PR is opened, so there is no
transition on them to miss. A reopened PR is already covered — reopening
moves `state` (CLOSED back to OPEN), which was already in the key. A PR's
base branch is not in the key because it is not in the row at all: Deck's
panel does not render it (see the field list two paragraphs up), so this
script does not capture it and there is nothing to go stale.

REPOSITORY LIST is derived from the project registry (`lib/agent-platform/
projects.py`) — every repo belonging to a registered, non-private project,
each expanded to its own `owner/repo` by `projects.repo_github_slug` (so a
repo registered under a non-`convivy` owner, like `homebrew-tap`, resolves to
its real slug rather than being read under the wrong owner's installation) —
rather than hardcoded here. A project's repos change as the registry changes,
and a hardcoded list would silently drift from it. Repos of `private: true`
(black-box, ADR-0043) and `walled_off: true` (platform/decisions/0100)
projects are excluded, the same wall each decision draws for its crew's other
output: none of their content reaches a shared store.

NOT IN SCOPE, AND NOT ABSORBED HERE: Orient's existing capture of PR reviews,
comments and the changelog feed, which still authenticates as the operator's personal account.
That is a separate, larger migration (platform/decisions/0051) and a different
item; this script covers pull-request STATE alone — WIDENED, 2026-11-02
(CQ·636, for Orient's merge-queue and code-health pages), to also cover two
sibling read-only shapes that reuse this same per-owner installation/token
loop rather than a third script: each open PR's `mergeable`/`review_decision`
(folded into the PR-state row itself — see `fetch_open_pr_decisions`), and
each CODE-HEALTH repo's (`projects.code_health_repos()`, a subset of this
script's own repo list) latest `ci.yml` workflow runs (`list_ci_runs`,
`rows_from_ci_runs`), captured as a second signal kind
(`_CI_RUN_KIND`/`_CI_RUN_STREAM`) with its own identity-diff dedup
(`_ci_run_identity`/`_changed_ci_runs`), mirroring the PR-row mechanism in a
separate state file per repo so the two kinds' delivered-state records never
collide or affect each other. The code-health phase mints a SEPARATE
installation token (`_CODE_HEALTH_PERMISSIONS`) rather than widening the
PR-state token every owner already depends on, so an owner whose
installation does not grant `actions: read` loses only its code-health
capture, logged, not its PR-state capture.

CQ·636's fourth item — each code-health repo's CI coverage figure — lands
here too, stacked on the PR that added the above: a third signal kind
(`_COVERAGE_KIND`/`_COVERAGE_STREAM`), read from the newest non-expired
`coverage`-named Actions artifact on each code-health repo
(`capture_coverage`), over the SAME `ch_token` the CI-run phase already
mints — `actions: read` already covers artifacts, not only workflow runs,
so no third token. Deduplicated on the artifact id alone, in its own state
file (`load_delivered_coverage`/`save_delivered_coverage`), mirroring the
CI-run mechanism. An owner whose installation grants `actions: read` but
refuses artifact reads on one particular repo (GitHub 403) is caught by
name (`ArtifactsPermissionDenied`) and logged, skipping only that repo's
coverage, the same posture `GA.NotInstalled` already gets for a whole
missing installation. See `capture_coverage`'s own docstring for the
untrusted-archive handling (a size cap before download, a capped read, an
in-memory zip parse that never extracts to disk, and a root-only,
suffix-filtered, size-capped choice of member) and `_NoRedirect`'s for why
the App's bearer token is never resent to the third-party URL GitHub's zip
endpoint redirects to.

AN ARTIFACT ONLY COUNTS WHEN IT RODE THE REPO'S DEFAULT BRANCH ON A
SUCCESSFUL `ci.yml` RUN (CQ·636, steward ruling 2026-11-02, fixing the
Library's CQ·253 plain latest-per-repo read: on 2026-11-02 23:41Z, 5 of 6
coverage rows Orient read were feature-branch builds, because this phase
used to take the newest unexpired `coverage` artifact with no filter at
all). `list_coverage_artifacts`'s own `workflow_run.head_branch` is
compared against `repo_default_branch`'s fresh `GET /repos/{repo}` read —
never assumed to be `main` — and the surviving candidates' runs are
checked newest-first via `_run_is_successful_ci_run` (`GET .../actions/
runs/{id}`, since the generic run endpoint is not scoped to one workflow
the way `list_ci_runs`'s `.../workflows/ci.yml/runs` URL already is, so
this checks the run's own `path`) until one is both a `ci.yml` run and
`conclusion == "success"`. This candidate walk is capped at
`_COVERAGE_RUN_CANDIDATES_MAX`: `list_ci_runs`'s own already-fetched runs
are NOT reused here, even though it reads the same repo moments earlier in
`main()`'s loop — its window (`_CI_RUN_WINDOW` latest, across all branches,
plus the same count filtered to the literal branch `"main"`) has no
guarantee of covering the specific run behind the newest default-branch
artifact, so treating a miss there as "does not qualify" would wrongly
skip a real candidate. `repo_default_branch` costs one GET per code-health
repo per pass, skipped entirely when `list_coverage_artifacts` already
found nothing to filter; each candidate run check costs at most one
further GET, stopping at the first qualifying candidate, so the common
case (the newest artifact is already on the default branch from a
successful run) costs exactly two GETs beyond the listing itself. No
qualifying candidate means no coverage row this pass (logged, not a
failure) — the existing rows the Library already has stand; this phase
never deletes or overwrites a row GitHub didn't in fact produce.

SIGNAL POSTING USES ITS OWN CREDENTIAL, SEPARATE FROM THE GITHUB APP TOKEN
ABOVE (issue_01M3VY56TWPFFHQ50QTFM5P07K). `POST /signals` rate-limits each
authenticated actor to 120/minute (library#176); this job alone peaked at
1,878 signals/minute, which would otherwise hold the whole shared
agent-platform bucket for minutes and starve every other sender's signals.
`signal_token()` resolves `PR_STATE_CAPTURE_TOKEN` or the 0600 file
`~/.orient-control/pr-state-capture-token`, the same resolution order
`signal_client.token()` uses for the shared credential. Until the Library
mints and installs that credential, `main()` falls back to the shared token
(via `post_signal`'s own `tok or token()`) and warns once to stderr.

Usage:
    pr-state-capture
"""
from __future__ import annotations

import argparse
import contextlib
import hashlib
import io
import json
import os
import sys
import time
import unicodedata
import urllib.parse
import urllib.request
import zipfile
from pathlib import Path

sys.path.insert(0, str(Path(__file__).resolve().parent.parent / "lib" / "agent-platform"))

import github_app as GA  # noqa: E402
import projects  # noqa: E402
import signal_client as SC  # noqa: E402

# The install's own GitHub App (issue_01M2KV16ZRG3N49GRER8Q7545H), installed on
# more than one GitHub owner (org-wide on `convivy`; also on `convivybuilder`,
# for homebrew-tap — see the module docstring's "ONE INSTALLATION PER OWNER"
# section). Resolved fresh through `GA.installed_app()`, never a literal. Each
# owner's installation is derived and checked on every run, never stored — the
# same posture kb_merge.knowledge_installation takes for convivy-library.

# The token this script mints can only ever read, whatever the App itself holds
# — see the module docstring's READ-ONLY section.
_READ_ONLY_PERMISSIONS = {"pull_requests": "read", "metadata": "read"}

# A SEPARATE token, minted only for owners that have at least one
# `code_health` repo (CQ·636) — never folded into `_READ_ONLY_PERMISSIONS`
# above. `actions: read` is a new ask this script never needed before; a
# second, narrower token means an installation that does not grant it costs
# only the code-health phase for that owner (logged, not fatal — see
# `main()`), rather than widening the PR-state token every owner already
# depends on and risking PR-state capture itself on a permission it never
# needed. `verdict`'s own `VERB_PERMISSIONS` entry in `github_app.py` already
# asks this same App for `actions: read` successfully, so this is using more
# of what the install already grants, not asking GitHub to grant it something
# new.
_CODE_HEALTH_PERMISSIONS = {"actions": "read", "metadata": "read"}

_KIND = "pr_state"
_STREAM = "pr-state"

# A code-health repo's captured `ci.yml` run, one signal per run id whose
# status or conclusion is new since the last post (CQ·636) — a different
# kind from `_KIND` above: this is a workflow run, not a pull request.
_CI_RUN_KIND = "ci_workflow_run"
_CI_RUN_STREAM = "ci-workflow-run"

# Orient's own CI_WINDOW (code_health_build.py), relayed by the steward
# 2026-11-02 superseding this item's original "trailing window" framing: a
# COUNT, not a time window. A quiet repo's latest 20 runs can reach back
# months; capturing by count rather than by age is what lets a quiet repo's
# code-health page show anything at all.
_CI_RUN_WINDOW = 20

# A code-health repo's own coverage figure (CQ·636's fourth item), one
# signal per artifact id whose id is new since the last post — a third kind,
# distinct from both `_KIND` and `_CI_RUN_KIND` above: this is a coverage
# reading, not a pull request or a workflow run.
_COVERAGE_KIND = "code_coverage"
_COVERAGE_STREAM = "code-coverage"

# The coverage artifact's own reported `size_in_bytes` must not exceed this
# before anything is downloaded (`_artifact_within_cap`) — refused outright,
# logged, with no coverage record for that repo this pass. A coverage.py or
# Istanbul-style JSON report, even for a large monorepo, is a few hundred KB;
# 20 MiB is headroom for a report nobody here has reason to expect, not a
# budget this is meant to spend.
_COVERAGE_ARTIFACT_MAX_BYTES = 20_000_000

# Independent of `size_in_bytes` above: the bytes actually read off the wire
# are capped at the same limit (`download_artifact_zip`), since the artifact
# is otherwise untrusted and could under-report its own size.
_COVERAGE_DOWNLOAD_MAX_BYTES = _COVERAGE_ARTIFACT_MAX_BYTES

# One zip member's own uncompressed size, capped far below the archive cap
# above (`parse_coverage_zip`) — a single coverage.json has no reason to
# approach the archive's own cap, and capping the DECOMPRESSED READ (not the
# entry's reported `file_size`) is what defuses a zip bomb: an entry that
# claims to be small can still decompress to far more than this if the read
# itself is not capped too.
_COVERAGE_MEMBER_MAX_BYTES = 5_000_000

# Bounds how many of a repo's own default-branch coverage artifacts
# `latest_qualifying_coverage_artifact` checks the run of (one GET each,
# `_run_is_successful_ci_run`) before giving up on this pass. The common
# case is 1: the newest default-branch artifact's own run already
# qualifies. A repo whose last several default-branch runs all failed
# costs up to this many GETs rather than one per artifact it has ever kept.
_COVERAGE_RUN_CANDIDATES_MAX = 5

# This job's OWN POST /signals credential (library#176 — 120 signals/minute is
# per authenticated actor, and this job alone peaked at 1,878/minute, so it
# needs its own actor and its own bucket rather than sharing the
# agent-platform token every other sender presents). Resolved the same way
# signal_client.token() resolves the shared one. Unrelated to the install's
# own GitHub App key (`GA.installed_app()`), which is for reading GitHub, not
# for posting signals.
SIGNAL_TOKEN_ENV = "PR_STATE_CAPTURE_TOKEN"
SIGNAL_TOKEN_FILE = Path.home() / ".orient-control" / "pr-state-capture-token"

# Send-side state: the last identity (see `_delivered_identity`) actually
# DELIVERED for each PR, kept so a pass does not re-POST a row nobody has
# touched since the last one (see the module docstring's section on this).
# It is state, not a cache — losing it does not corrupt anything (the
# store's own `dedup_key` is still the correctness backstop), but it does
# re-flood the Library with every PR this repo has, so it is written with
# the same care `limit_resume.save_state` and `access_assertion`'s JWKS
# cache give their own `~/.local/state/agent-platform/...` records: the
# directory 0700, the file 0600, written atomically.
STATE_DIR = Path.home() / ".local" / "state" / "agent-platform" / "pr-state-capture"


def signal_token() -> str | None:
    """`SIGNAL_TOKEN_ENV` or the 0600 file `SIGNAL_TOKEN_FILE`, or None when
    neither is set — until the Library mints and installs this job's own
    credential, `main()` falls back to the shared agent-platform token
    (`signal_client.token()`, via `post_signal`'s own `tok or token()`) and
    warns once to stderr that it is doing so."""
    tok = os.environ.get(SIGNAL_TOKEN_ENV, "").strip()
    if tok:
        return tok
    try:
        return SIGNAL_TOKEN_FILE.read_text().strip() or None
    except OSError:
        return None


# Bounds how long one capture run keeps resending a SINGLE signal into the
# Library's POST /signals 429 (library#176, 120 signals/minute/submitter —
# see signal_client.post_signal's 429 handling) before main() gives up on it
# and stops the rest of the pass (see main()'s own 429 handling below). This
# run's own StartInterval is 180s (launchd/com.convivy.pr-state-capture.plist);
# a pass that runs long has its next scheduled fire skipped rather than
# overlapping it (launchd.plist(5), StartInterval), so 600s bounds how long a
# stuck signal is retried, not a race against the next fire.
#
# A prior version of this comment claimed a 300-signal run drains in about
# 15s at 120/minute. That arithmetic was wrong: 300 signals at 120/minute
# take 150s (2.5 minutes). It also understated the real passes this was
# meant to cover — the 2026-10-01 incident that prompted the send-only-
# what-changed fix (see the module docstring) found one pass attempting
# over 4,000 rows, which at 120/minute cannot drain inside any deadline this
# short. Post-fix, a steady-state pass is the handful of PRs that actually
# changed since the last one, not the whole inventory, so 600s is headroom
# for an ordinary pass rather than a budget it is expected to spend.
_RUN_DEADLINE_SECONDS = 600

_PAGE_SIZE = 100
# Bounds one repo's read to 1000 PRs per pass, so a large repo degrades to "this
# pass saw its newest-updated 1000" (logged) rather than an unbounded page loop.
# The busiest product repo passed it on 2026-09-16 with 1,135 PRs. The cap then
# fell at 2026-05-08 while its least recently updated open PR was touched
# 2026-09-14, so only long-closed PRs were left out; an open PR idle past the
# cap would be too.
_MAX_PAGES = 10


def repos_to_capture() -> list[str]:
    """`owner/repo` for every repo of a registered project that is neither
    private (ADR-0043's wall) nor walled off (platform/decisions/0100), each
    expanded by `projects.repo_github_slug` — the one expansion every other
    owner/repo call site in this repository uses, so a repo registered under
    a non-`convivy` owner (homebrew-tap, under Builder's own `convivybuilder`
    account) resolves to its real slug rather than defaulting to `convivy`.

    `private_slugs()` and `walled_off_slugs()` are read in one `try`, the
    same fail-closed step `run-register-api` reads them in on every route:
    either raising returns [] rather than a subset computed against a
    partial exclusion set, since this list feeds data this process posts
    outward."""
    try:
        excluded = projects.private_slugs() | projects.walled_off_slugs()
    except Exception:
        return []
    out = {projects.repo_github_slug(r, unit)
           for slug, unit in projects.load().items() if slug not in excluded
           for r in unit.get("repos", [])}
    return sorted(out)


def _by_owner(repos: list[str]) -> dict[str, list[str]]:
    """*repos* (each already an `owner/repo` slug) grouped by owner, each
    group's repos kept in *repos*' own order — `main()` resolves one
    installation and mints one token per owner, rather than per run."""
    out: dict[str, list[str]] = {}
    for repo in repos:
        out.setdefault(repo.split("/", 1)[0], []).append(repo)
    return out


def installed_repos(token: str, *, opener=urllib.request.urlopen) -> set[str]:
    """Lowercased `owner/repo` for every repository the installation reaches.

    Read so `main` can tell a registered repo that has no GitHub copy from one
    the App cannot read. GitHub answers 404 for both, and on 2026-09-16 five
    registered repos (local-only checkouts, or projects never pushed) turned
    every pass into exit 1, which hides a real failure behind a permanent one."""
    out: set[str] = set()
    for page in range(1, _MAX_PAGES + 1):
        code, payload = GA.request(
            "GET", f"{GA.API}/installation/repositories?per_page={_PAGE_SIZE}&page={page}",
            token, None, opener=opener)
        repos = payload.get("repositories") if isinstance(payload, dict) else None
        if code != 200 or not isinstance(repos, list):
            raise GA.Unreadable(f"the installation's repositories could not be read ({code})")
        out.update((r.get("full_name") or "").lower() for r in repos)
        if len(repos) < _PAGE_SIZE:
            return out
    raise GA.Unreadable(f"the installation lists more than {_MAX_PAGES * _PAGE_SIZE} "
                        f"repositories; refusing to treat an unread page as absence")


def list_pulls(repo: str, token: str, *, opener=urllib.request.urlopen) -> list[dict]:
    """Every pull request in `repo`, newest-updated first. `state=all` is what
    surfaces a recently-closed or recently-merged PR without a second query."""
    out: list[dict] = []
    for page in range(1, _MAX_PAGES + 1):
        code, payload = GA.request(
            "GET",
            f"{GA.API}/repos/{repo}/pulls?state=all&sort=updated&direction=desc"
            f"&per_page={_PAGE_SIZE}&page={page}",
            token, None, opener=opener)
        if code != 200 or not isinstance(payload, list):
            raise GA.Unreadable(f"{repo}: pull requests could not be read ({code})")
        out.extend(payload)
        if len(payload) < _PAGE_SIZE:
            return out
    print(f"pr-state-capture: {repo}: stopped at the {_MAX_PAGES * _PAGE_SIZE}-PR page "
          f"cap for this pass; older PRs are not captured this time", file=sys.stderr)
    return out


# One GraphQL call (paginated past 100 open PRs) rather than a REST GET per
# open PR for `mergeable_state` plus a second source for `review_decision`:
# REST's list endpoint omits `mergeable_state` entirely (only the single-PR
# GET carries it), and REST has no `review_decision` field at all — GitHub
# computes it server-side from branch-protection rules a read-only
# `pull_requests: read` token cannot itself re-derive. GraphQL's
# `mergeStateStatus` is GitHub's own GraphQL name for the exact value REST
# calls `mergeable_state` (the same value space: clean, dirty, blocked,
# behind, unstable, draft, has_hooks, unknown — see Orient's own correction
# on this issue, CQ·636, flagging that the route must say which vocabulary
# it serves: this is REST's, read through GraphQL's equivalent field).
_DECISIONS_QUERY = """
query($owner: String!, $name: String!, $after: String) {
  repository(owner: $owner, name: $name) {
    pullRequests(states: OPEN, first: 100, after: $after) {
      nodes { number mergeStateStatus reviewDecision }
      pageInfo { hasNextPage endCursor }
    }
  }
}
"""


def fetch_open_pr_decisions(repo: str, token: str,
                            *, opener=urllib.request.urlopen) -> dict[int, dict]:
    """{number: {"mergeable": str | None, "review_decision": str | None}} for
    every pull request GitHub currently reports OPEN on `repo` — see
    `_DECISIONS_QUERY`'s comment for why this is one GraphQL call rather than
    a REST read.

    `mergeable` folds GraphQL's `"UNKNOWN"` (lowercased: `"unknown"`) to
    `None`: GitHub computes this lazily, so a brand-new PR's first read can
    come back with no real answer yet. Recording `None` rather than retrying
    in a loop is deliberate (CQ·636) — the NEXT capture pass rereads it, and
    `_changed_rows`'s identity diff (which now includes both fields) emits
    the real value the moment GitHub resolves it, the same self-heal
    `_changed_rows` already gives a missed pass. `review_decision` passes
    `reviewDecision` straight through (`None`, `"APPROVED"`,
    `"CHANGES_REQUESTED"` or `"REVIEW_REQUIRED"` — GraphQL's own enum, no
    translation needed)."""
    owner, name = repo.split("/", 1)
    out: dict[int, dict] = {}
    after = None
    for _ in range(_MAX_PAGES):
        data = GA.graphql(_DECISIONS_QUERY, {"owner": owner, "name": name, "after": after},
                          token, opener=opener)
        conn = ((data.get("repository") or {}).get("pullRequests") or {})
        for node in conn.get("nodes") or []:
            number = node.get("number")
            if number is None:
                continue
            state = (node.get("mergeStateStatus") or "").lower()
            out[number] = {
                "mergeable": state if state and state != "unknown" else None,
                "review_decision": node.get("reviewDecision"),
            }
        page_info = conn.get("pageInfo") or {}
        if not page_info.get("hasNextPage"):
            return out
        after = page_info.get("endCursor")
    print(f"pr-state-capture: {repo}: stopped at the {_MAX_PAGES}-page GraphQL cap "
          f"reading open PR decisions this pass; some open PRs' mergeable/"
          f"review_decision are not captured this time", file=sys.stderr)
    return out


def list_ci_runs(repo: str, token: str, *, branch: str | None = None,
                 opener=urllib.request.urlopen) -> list[dict]:
    """The latest `_CI_RUN_WINDOW` `ci.yml` runs for `repo` — across every
    branch when `branch` is None, else only on `branch` — whatever their age
    (a COUNT window, not a time one; see `_CI_RUN_WINDOW`'s comment). One
    GET, no pagination: `per_page` IS the window."""
    qs = f"per_page={_CI_RUN_WINDOW}"
    if branch:
        qs += f"&branch={urllib.parse.quote(branch)}"
    code, payload = GA.request(
        "GET", f"{GA.API}/repos/{repo}/actions/workflows/ci.yml/runs?{qs}",
        token, None, opener=opener)
    runs = payload.get("workflow_runs") if isinstance(payload, dict) else None
    if code != 200 or not isinstance(runs, list):
        raise GA.Unreadable(f"{repo}: ci.yml runs could not be read ({code})")
    return runs


def rows_from_ci_runs(repo: str, runs: list[dict]) -> list[dict]:
    """One row per run id, deduplicated across however many `list_ci_runs`
    reads were merged into `runs` (a `main` run appears in both the
    all-branches and the main-only read, so the caller passing both through
    here must not double-count it).

    `branch` (`head_branch`) and `event` are GitHub-authored free text, the
    same untrusted-output contract `rows_from_pulls` enforces for `title`/
    `author` (see that function's docstring): any collaborator who can push a
    branch controls `head_branch`, and `event` reflects `repository_dispatch`'s
    caller-supplied `event_type` for a run triggered that way. Both go through
    `SC.sanitise` here, at the one point this script turns GitHub's payload
    into its own row, so `emit_ci_run`'s body string and `meta` dict only ever
    see the sanitised value. `status`/`conclusion` are not sanitised: GitHub
    draws both from its own fixed vocabulary, never from caller-supplied
    text. `head_sha` is not sanitised either, for the reason `rows_from_pulls`
    gives: git constrains an object id to lowercase hex."""
    by_id: dict[int, dict] = {}
    for run in runs:
        run_id = run.get("id")
        if run_id is None:
            continue
        by_id[run_id] = {
            "repo": repo,
            "run_id": run_id,
            "branch": SC.sanitise(run.get("head_branch") or "", max_len=200),
            "head_sha": run.get("head_sha") or "",
            "event": SC.sanitise(run.get("event") or "", max_len=200),
            "status": run.get("status") or "",
            "conclusion": run.get("conclusion"),
            "created_at": run.get("created_at") or "",
            "updated_at": run.get("updated_at") or "",
        }
    return list(by_id.values())


def _state(pr: dict) -> str:
    """OPEN | CLOSED | MERGED — the tri-state agentctl's own `pr_state` reads
    from `gh`, so Deck sees the same vocabulary a PR has everywhere else on this
    platform. The raw REST list endpoint only ever reports `state` as open/closed
    plus a `merged_at` timestamp; merged is folded in here rather than left for
    a reader to infer from two fields."""
    if pr.get("merged_at"):
        return "MERGED"
    return (pr.get("state") or "").upper()


def _identity_title(raw_title: str) -> str:
    """The identity form of a title: two titles a human reads as the SAME
    text must produce the SAME string here, so `title_hash` cannot be churned
    by an edit no reader would notice (security-reviewer, #595 rounds 3-5).

    Starts from `SC.sanitise`'s output (NFC-normalised, ANSI/control
    characters stripped, credentials redacted, whitespace collapsed, and the
    DISPLAY-safe subset of Default_Ignorable codepoints already gone — see
    `signal_client._DISPLAY_KEEP_CODEPOINTS`), NFKC-folds it (round 3's
    non-blocking finding: NFC alone leaves *visible* compatibility-equivalent
    forms distinct — a fullwidth "Ａ" from an ASCII "A", the "ﬁ" ligature
    from "fi", a circled "①" from "1" — because folding those changes what
    the text actually SAYS, out of scope for `sanitise`, which every OTHER
    caller uses to render text for display unmodified), THEN removes every
    remaining Default_Ignorable_Code_Point via `signal_client.
    is_default_ignorable` directly — with no keep list, unlike `sanitise`.

    That last step is IDENTITY's split from DISPLAY (round 5, #595): display
    now deliberately keeps the zero-width joiner, the variation selectors,
    the zero-width non-joiner and the soft hyphen, because removing any of
    them can change what a reader sees (a ZWJ-joined family emoji splitting
    into four separate people; a gitmoji recycle symbol losing its forced
    emoji presentation — reviewer, round 4). Identity has no such contract:
    two titles differing only by one of THOSE codepoints still read as the
    same title to a human skimming Deck's panel, so `title_hash` must not
    change either, even though `row["title"]` (display) now visibly does. A
    final whitespace collapse is a defensive no-op for every character NFKC
    or this strip removes today (none introduces new whitespace) and costs
    nothing to keep in case a future compatibility mapping ever does."""
    folded = unicodedata.normalize("NFKC", SC.sanitise(raw_title))
    folded = "".join(ch for ch in folded if not SC.is_default_ignorable(ch))
    return " ".join(folded.split())


def rows_from_pulls(repo: str, pulls: list[dict]) -> list[dict]:
    """One row per pull, the exact fields Deck's panel needs.

    `title` and `author` are GitHub-authored free text (any collaborator can
    set a PR's title; `author` is the login GitHub reports for whoever opened
    it) — the same untrusted-output-not-platform-authored contract every other
    `sanitise` call in this codebase enforces (see signal_client.sanitise's
    module docstring; bin/issue-pr-reconcile's `title` extraction is the same
    shape). Both pass through `SC.sanitise` here, at the one point this script
    turns GitHub's payload into its own row, so every downstream reader (the
    emitted body string and the `meta` dict alike) only ever sees the
    sanitised value. `head_sha` is not sanitised: it comes back from the same
    payload but git constrains an object id to lowercase hex, so there is no
    free-text surface to sanitise.

    `title_hash` is a hash of `_identity_title(raw_title)`, not of the 200-char
    display `title` and not of plain `SC.sanitise` output — see the module
    docstring's section on the signal's own identity and `_identity_title`'s
    own docstring for why `dedup_key_for` keys on this instead of either of
    those.

    `created_at` and `merged_at` (CQ·636, for Orient's open-PR-age and
    merged-count figures) come straight off the same list-endpoint payload
    this function already reads — GitHub includes both there, so no second
    read. Neither moves after a PR is created (GitHub never un-merges one),
    so neither belongs in the dedup identity (`_delivered_identity`) the way
    `head_sha`/`state`/`draft`/`title_hash` do; a PR's `merged_at` going from
    falsy to set always arrives together with `state` moving to `MERGED`,
    already in that identity.

    `mergeable` and `review_decision` default to `None` here — this function
    reads only the list-endpoint payload, which carries neither (see
    `fetch_open_pr_decisions`'s docstring). `main()` overlays the real values
    for every OPEN row after a separate GraphQL read; a CLOSED or MERGED
    row's `mergeable`/`review_decision` has no meaning and stays `None`."""
    rows = []
    for pr in pulls:
        raw_title = pr.get("title") or ""
        identity_title = _identity_title(raw_title)
        rows.append({
            "repo": repo,
            "number": pr.get("number"),
            "title": SC.sanitise(raw_title, max_len=200),
            "title_hash": hashlib.sha256(identity_title.encode("utf-8")).hexdigest()[:16],
            "author": SC.sanitise(((pr.get("user") or {}).get("login")) or "?", max_len=100),
            "draft": bool(pr.get("draft")),
            "head_sha": ((pr.get("head") or {}).get("sha")) or "",
            "state": _state(pr),
            "updated_at": pr.get("updated_at") or "",
            "created_at": pr.get("created_at") or "",
            "merged_at": pr.get("merged_at") or None,
            "mergeable": None,
            "review_decision": None,
        })
    return rows


def _delivered_identity(row: dict) -> dict:
    """The subset of *row* that `dedup_key_for` keys on, as a plain dict a
    JSON record can compare against byte for byte. Everything else in *row*
    (`title`, `author`, `updated_at`, `created_at`, `merged_at`) can change
    with no transition `dedup_key_for` would recognise, so it must not make
    `_changed_rows` treat the row as changed when it isn't.

    `mergeable` and `review_decision` (CQ·636) ARE in this identity: a
    change in either must count as a change and re-post, per this item's own
    spec. Deploying this addition makes every row with a non-`None` value in
    either field look "changed" on the first pass after rollout (an old
    delivered-state record has no entry for either key, so the comparison
    differs) — a one-time full re-emit per affected repo, the same self-heal
    property a missing or wiped `STATE_DIR` already has (see the module
    docstring)."""
    return {"head_sha": row["head_sha"], "state": row["state"],
            "draft": row["draft"], "title_hash": row["title_hash"],
            "mergeable": row["mergeable"], "review_decision": row["review_decision"]}


def _changed_rows(rows: list[dict], delivered: dict) -> list[dict]:
    """*rows* (one repo's) whose identity differs from what *delivered*
    (that repo's `load_delivered` result) last recorded. A PR with no entry
    at all counts as changed too — the same "nothing delivered yet" case
    `load_delivered` itself falls back to."""
    return [row for row in rows
           if delivered.get(str(row["number"])) != _delivered_identity(row)]


def _state_path(repo: str) -> Path:
    return STATE_DIR / f"{repo.replace('/', '__')}.json"


def load_delivered(repo: str) -> dict:
    """The last-delivered identity per PR number for *repo*, keyed by the PR
    number as a string (JSON object keys are always strings), or `{}` with
    one stderr line when nothing has been recorded yet or the record cannot
    be read. Both cases mean "nothing delivered yet" to the rest of main(),
    which then emits every row for *repo* this pass — see the module
    docstring for why that self-heals rather than risking a duplicate."""
    path = _state_path(repo)
    try:
        raw = path.read_text()
    except OSError:
        print(f"pr-state-capture: {repo}: no delivered-state record yet; "
              f"emitting every PR for this repo this pass", file=sys.stderr)
        return {}
    try:
        data = json.loads(raw)
    except ValueError:
        print(f"pr-state-capture: {repo}: delivered-state record at {path} is "
              f"corrupt; emitting every PR for this repo this pass", file=sys.stderr)
        return {}
    return data if isinstance(data, dict) else {}


def save_delivered(repo: str, delivered: dict) -> None:
    """Atomic write of *delivered* for *repo*: a pid-unique temp file plus
    `os.replace`, mirroring `limit_resume.save_state`, so a reader never sees
    a half-written file. Called at most once per repo per pass (see main()),
    not once per PR."""
    STATE_DIR.mkdir(mode=0o700, parents=True, exist_ok=True)
    os.chmod(STATE_DIR, 0o700)  # mkdir's mode is a no-op on an already-existing dir
    path = _state_path(repo)
    tmp = path.with_name(f"{path.name}.{os.getpid()}.tmp")
    try:
        tmp.unlink()
    except OSError:
        pass
    fd = os.open(tmp, os.O_WRONLY | os.O_CREAT | os.O_EXCL, 0o600)
    with os.fdopen(fd, "w") as fh:
        fh.write(json.dumps(delivered, sort_keys=True))
    os.replace(tmp, path)


def _ci_run_identity(row: dict) -> dict:
    """The subset of a CI-run row that counts as a transition: a run's
    `status`/`conclusion` moving (queued -> in_progress -> completed,
    success/failure/cancelled/...) is the only thing this item's spec asks
    to be re-posted on. `branch`, `head_sha`, `event`, `created_at` and
    `updated_at` are immutable or derivable from the run id alone once it
    exists, so none belongs here."""
    return {"status": row["status"], "conclusion": row["conclusion"]}


def _changed_ci_runs(rows: list[dict], delivered: dict) -> list[dict]:
    """*rows* (one repo's captured `ci.yml` runs) whose status or conclusion
    differs from what *delivered* (`load_delivered_ci_runs`'s result) last
    recorded — mirrors `_changed_rows`'s identity-diff dedup for PR rows,
    keyed by run id instead of PR number."""
    return [row for row in rows
           if delivered.get(str(row["run_id"])) != _ci_run_identity(row)]


def _ci_run_state_path(repo: str) -> Path:
    return STATE_DIR / f"{repo.replace('/', '__')}__ci-runs.json"


def load_delivered_ci_runs(repo: str) -> dict:
    """The last-delivered `_ci_run_identity` per run id for *repo*, keyed by
    the run id as a string, or `{}` with one stderr line when nothing has
    been recorded yet or the record cannot be read — mirrors
    `load_delivered`'s self-heal for the PR-row state file, in a separate
    file (`_ci_run_state_path`) so a corrupt or missing CI-run record never
    affects PR-row delivery state or vice versa."""
    path = _ci_run_state_path(repo)
    try:
        raw = path.read_text()
    except OSError:
        print(f"pr-state-capture: {repo}: no delivered CI-run record yet; "
              f"emitting every captured run for this repo this pass", file=sys.stderr)
        return {}
    try:
        data = json.loads(raw)
    except ValueError:
        print(f"pr-state-capture: {repo}: delivered CI-run record at {path} is "
              f"corrupt; emitting every captured run for this repo this pass",
              file=sys.stderr)
        return {}
    return data if isinstance(data, dict) else {}


def save_delivered_ci_runs(repo: str, delivered: dict) -> None:
    """Atomic write of *delivered* for *repo*'s CI runs — mirrors
    `save_delivered`'s atomic write, into the separate CI-run state file."""
    STATE_DIR.mkdir(mode=0o700, parents=True, exist_ok=True)
    os.chmod(STATE_DIR, 0o700)
    path = _ci_run_state_path(repo)
    tmp = path.with_name(f"{path.name}.{os.getpid()}.tmp")
    try:
        tmp.unlink()
    except OSError:
        pass
    fd = os.open(tmp, os.O_WRONLY | os.O_CREAT | os.O_EXCL, 0o600)
    with os.fdopen(fd, "w") as fh:
        fh.write(json.dumps(delivered, sort_keys=True))
    os.replace(tmp, path)


def ci_run_dedup_key_for(row: dict) -> str:
    """The transition identity for a captured CI run: a repeated capture of
    the SAME (repo, run id, status, conclusion) reuses this key, mirroring
    `dedup_key_for`'s role for PR rows."""
    return (f"ci-workflow-run:{row['repo']}:{row['run_id']}:"
            f"{row['status']}:{row['conclusion'] or 'none'}")


# --- coverage (CQ·636's fourth item) -----------------------------------------


class ArtifactsPermissionDenied(Exception):
    """GitHub answered 403 reading *repo*'s Actions artifacts — a narrower
    refusal than `GA.Unreadable`: the App's installation does not grant
    this repo artifact read access, a confirmed permission gap rather than
    a failed read. `main()` catches this ahead of `GA.Unreadable`, the same
    posture `GA.NotInstalled` already gets for an owner with no
    installation at all (see the module docstring's "AN OWNER THE APP IS
    NOT INSTALLED ON IS SKIPPED, NOT FAILED" section) — logged by name and
    skipped, widening nothing, rather than failing the pass."""


def list_coverage_artifacts(repo: str, token: str,
                            *, opener=urllib.request.urlopen) -> list[dict]:
    """Every non-expired artifact named `coverage` for *repo*, paginated the
    same way `list_pulls`/`installed_repos` are (`_MAX_PAGES`/`_PAGE_SIZE`).
    Each entry carries its own `workflow_run` object (`id`, `head_sha`,
    `head_branch`) straight from this one read, so `capture_coverage` never
    makes a second call to resolve the run that produced it.

    Raises `ArtifactsPermissionDenied` on a 403 and `GA.Unreadable` on any
    other non-200."""
    out: list[dict] = []
    for page in range(1, _MAX_PAGES + 1):
        code, payload = GA.request(
            "GET", f"{GA.API}/repos/{repo}/actions/artifacts?per_page={_PAGE_SIZE}&page={page}",
            token, None, opener=opener)
        if code == 403:
            raise ArtifactsPermissionDenied(
                f"{repo}: the App cannot read Actions artifacts here (403)")
        artifacts = payload.get("artifacts") if isinstance(payload, dict) else None
        if code != 200 or not isinstance(artifacts, list):
            raise GA.Unreadable(f"{repo}: artifacts could not be read ({code})")
        out.extend(a for a in artifacts
                   if a.get("name") == "coverage" and not a.get("expired"))
        if len(artifacts) < _PAGE_SIZE:
            return out
    print(f"pr-state-capture: {repo}: stopped at the {_MAX_PAGES * _PAGE_SIZE}-artifact "
          f"page cap for this pass; an older coverage artifact is not considered "
          f"this time", file=sys.stderr)
    return out


def repo_default_branch(repo: str, token: str, *, opener=urllib.request.urlopen) -> str:
    """*repo*'s default branch, read fresh (`GET /repos/{repo}`) rather
    than assumed to be `main` (CQ·636) — a coverage artifact only counts
    when it rode this exact branch (`latest_qualifying_coverage_artifact`).

    Raises `GA.Unreadable` on a non-200, or when the response carries no
    non-empty `default_branch` string — GitHub's documented repository
    response always has one for a repository that exists at all, so a
    response missing it means this read could not be trusted, not that the
    repo happens to have no default branch."""
    code, payload = GA.request("GET", f"{GA.API}/repos/{repo}", token, None, opener=opener)
    branch = payload.get("default_branch") if isinstance(payload, dict) else None
    if code != 200 or not isinstance(branch, str) or not branch:
        raise GA.Unreadable(f"{repo}: default branch could not be read ({code})")
    return branch


def _run_is_successful_ci_run(repo: str, run_id: int, token: str,
                              *, opener=urllib.request.urlopen) -> bool:
    """Whether workflow run *run_id* on *repo* is a `ci.yml` run whose
    conclusion is `success` — read fresh via the generic run endpoint
    (`GET /repos/{repo}/actions/runs/{run_id}`). That endpoint is not
    scoped to one workflow the way `list_ci_runs`'s own `.../workflows/
    ci.yml/runs` URL already is, so the run's own `path` (e.g.
    `.github/workflows/ci.yml`) is checked here instead.

    Raises `GA.Unreadable` on a non-200 — including a 404, since that
    reflects a read this pass could not complete (the run vanished between
    the artifact listing and this check), not a confirmed non-match."""
    code, payload = GA.request(
        "GET", f"{GA.API}/repos/{repo}/actions/runs/{run_id}", token, None, opener=opener)
    if code != 200 or not isinstance(payload, dict):
        raise GA.Unreadable(f"{repo}: run {run_id} could not be read ({code})")
    path = payload.get("path") or ""
    return path.rsplit("/", 1)[-1] == "ci.yml" and payload.get("conclusion") == "success"


def latest_qualifying_coverage_artifact(
    repo: str, artifacts: list[dict], default_branch: str, token: str,
    *, opener=urllib.request.urlopen,
) -> dict | None:
    """The newest of *artifacts* whose `workflow_run.head_branch` is
    *default_branch* AND whose run is a successful `ci.yml` run, or None
    when no candidate qualifies — `capture_coverage`'s "take the newest
    qualifying one" step (CQ·636, fixing the Library's plain
    latest-per-repo read, CQ·253, from posting a feature-branch build or a
    failed default-branch run as a repo's current coverage figure).

    Candidates are *artifacts* filtered to `default_branch`, walked
    newest-`created_at`-first; each costs at most one run check
    (`_run_is_successful_ci_run`) until the first qualifies, capped at
    `_COVERAGE_RUN_CANDIDATES_MAX` candidates actually checked — see that
    constant's comment for why the window is bounded rather than
    exhaustive."""
    candidates = sorted(
        (a for a in artifacts
         if ((a.get("workflow_run") or {}).get("head_branch")) == default_branch),
        key=lambda a: a.get("created_at") or "", reverse=True,
    )
    for artifact in candidates[:_COVERAGE_RUN_CANDIDATES_MAX]:
        run_id = (artifact.get("workflow_run") or {}).get("id")
        if run_id is None:
            continue
        if _run_is_successful_ci_run(repo, run_id, token, opener=opener):
            return artifact
    return None


def _artifact_within_cap(artifact: dict, *, max_bytes: int) -> bool:
    """Whether *artifact*'s own reported `size_in_bytes` is small enough to
    download at all — checked BEFORE any download, never raising. A
    `size_in_bytes` that is absent, a bool, negative or not a number is
    treated as exceeding the cap: an artifact this script cannot vouch for
    the size of is refused the same as one confirmed too large, rather than
    downloaded on trust."""
    size = artifact.get("size_in_bytes")
    if isinstance(size, bool) or not isinstance(size, (int, float)):
        return False
    return 0 <= size <= max_bytes


class _NoRedirect(urllib.request.HTTPRedirectHandler):
    """Refuses to auto-follow a redirect — used only for the artifact-zip
    download below. GitHub answers that endpoint with a 302 to its own
    time-limited, pre-signed download URL; `urlopen`'s DEFAULT redirect
    handling resends every request header, Authorization included, to
    wherever a redirect points, host included (CPython's
    `HTTPRedirectHandler.redirect_request` copies `req.headers` verbatim
    bar `content-length`/`content-type`; it does not drop `Authorization`
    on a cross-host redirect). Returning None here refuses the redirect;
    `download_artifact_zip` then follows it BY HAND, over a plain,
    unauthenticated request, so this App's bearer token is never sent to
    that second host."""

    def redirect_request(self, *args, **kwargs):
        return None


# Built once, at import time — this handler carries no per-request state, so
# one opener instance is reused by every call rather than rebuilt per request.
_ZIP_OPENER = urllib.request.build_opener(_NoRedirect).open


def _get_raw(url: str, auth: str | None, *, opener, max_bytes: int) -> tuple[int, object, bytes]:
    """One GET, read as raw bytes rather than JSON (`GA.request`'s own
    shape, for a caller that needs the body unparsed) — the shared half of
    `download_artifact_zip`'s two requests (the signed zip endpoint, then,
    on a redirect, the download URL it points to). Capped at
    `max_bytes + 1` bytes actually read, on either the success path's
    `resp.read()` or a non-2xx response's own `e.read()`, since a redirect
    response or an error page can be oversized too. Returns
    `(status, headers, body)` for every response GitHub (or the redirect
    target) answers with — a non-2xx arrives as `urllib.error.HTTPError`
    here exactly as it does through plain `urlopen`, and is folded back
    into this same three-tuple shape rather than left to propagate, so the
    one caller below can read a 302's `Location` off `headers` whichever
    path produced it."""
    headers = {"X-GitHub-Api-Version": "2022-11-28"}
    if auth:
        headers["Authorization"] = auth
        headers["Accept"] = "application/vnd.github+json"
    req = urllib.request.Request(url, headers=headers)
    try:
        with opener(req, timeout=30) as resp:
            return resp.status, resp.headers, resp.read(max_bytes + 1)
    except urllib.error.HTTPError as e:
        try:
            body = e.read(max_bytes + 1)
        except OSError:
            body = b""
        return e.code, e.headers, body


def download_artifact_zip(repo: str, artifact_id: int, token: str, *,
                          max_bytes: int, opener=_ZIP_OPENER) -> bytes | None:
    """The artifact's zip archive, over the REST zip endpoint
    (`GET repos/<repo>/actions/artifacts/<id>/zip`) — never `gh`. `opener`
    defaults to `_ZIP_OPENER`, which refuses to auto-follow GitHub's 302 to
    its own pre-signed download URL; this function follows it itself, over
    a SECOND, unauthenticated request (see `_NoRedirect`'s docstring for
    why the App's bearer token must not reach that second host).

    Returns None, never raising, the moment more than `max_bytes` bytes
    have actually been read off either response's body — independent of
    what the artifact's own `size_in_bytes` claimed (`_artifact_within_cap`
    is the check against THAT; this is the read-time backstop against an
    artifact that under-reports its own size). Raises `GA.Unreadable` on a
    redirect with no `Location`, or on any response that is neither a
    redirect nor 200."""
    url = f"{GA.API}/repos/{repo}/actions/artifacts/{artifact_id}/zip"
    status, headers, body = _get_raw(url, f"Bearer {token}", opener=opener, max_bytes=max_bytes)
    if status in (301, 302, 303, 307):
        location = headers.get("Location") if headers else None
        if not location:
            raise GA.Unreadable(
                f"{repo}: artifact {artifact_id} zip redirected with no Location")
        status, _, body = _get_raw(location, None, opener=opener, max_bytes=max_bytes)
    if status != 200:
        raise GA.Unreadable(f"{repo}: artifact {artifact_id} zip could not be read ({status})")
    if len(body) > max_bytes:
        return None
    return body


def _coverage_percent_from_json(obj) -> tuple[float | None, str | None]:
    """The 0-100 coverage percentage *obj* (already-parsed JSON) reports,
    and which of coverage.py's native shape or the `pct` shape produced it
    — or `(None, None)` when neither shape is present, the value is not a
    number, the value falls outside 0 to 100, or *obj* is not even a JSON
    object. Matches Orient's own two accepted shapes exactly
    (code_health_build.py, relayed in CQ·636's body), including which it
    tries first."""
    if not isinstance(obj, dict):
        return None, None
    totals = obj.get("totals")
    if isinstance(totals, dict) and "percent_covered" in totals:
        value, source = totals["percent_covered"], "totals.percent_covered"
    elif "pct" in obj:
        value, source = obj["pct"], "pct"
    else:
        return None, None
    if isinstance(value, bool) or not isinstance(value, (int, float)):
        return None, None
    if not (0 <= value <= 100):
        return None, None
    return float(value), source


def parse_coverage_zip(data: bytes,
                       *, member_max_bytes: int) -> tuple[float | None, str | None]:
    """The 0-100 coverage percentage inside *data* (an artifact's zip
    archive, downloaded and otherwise untrusted), and which JSON shape
    produced it, or `(None, None)` on ANY refusal or parse failure — never
    raises. Refused, each silently folding into that same `(None, None)`:

    - *data* is not a valid zip (`zipfile.BadZipFile`);
    - no member sits at the archive root (no `/` or `\\` in its name — a
      nested path and a traversal-looking name are both excluded this way;
      a zip directory entry's name always ends in `/`, so this same check
      excludes one too, with nothing further needed) AND ends in `.json`;
    - every such candidate's own reported `file_size` exceeds
      `member_max_bytes`;
    - the chosen member (named exactly `coverage.json` if one candidate is,
      else the first candidate in the archive's own order) decompresses to
      more than `member_max_bytes` bytes once actually read — capped on the
      READ itself (`ZipExtFile.read(member_max_bytes + 1)`), not on the
      entry's own reported size, since a crafted archive can under-report
      that and still decompress to far more (a zip bomb);
    - the chosen member's bytes do not parse as JSON, or parse but match
      neither of `_coverage_percent_from_json`'s two shapes.

    Reads ONLY the chosen member, in memory (`ZipFile.open`/`.read`, never
    `extract`/`extractall` — nothing here ever touches disk)."""
    try:
        zf = zipfile.ZipFile(io.BytesIO(data))
    except zipfile.BadZipFile:
        return None, None
    try:
        candidates = [info for info in zf.infolist()
                     if "/" not in info.filename
                     and "\\" not in info.filename
                     and info.filename.endswith(".json")
                     and info.file_size <= member_max_bytes]
        if not candidates:
            return None, None
        chosen = next((c for c in candidates if c.filename == "coverage.json"), candidates[0])
        with zf.open(chosen) as fh:
            raw = fh.read(member_max_bytes + 1)
        if len(raw) > member_max_bytes:
            return None, None
        try:
            obj = json.loads(raw)
        except ValueError:
            return None, None
        return _coverage_percent_from_json(obj)
    finally:
        zf.close()


def rows_from_coverage(repo: str, artifact: dict, percent: float, source: str) -> dict:
    """The one row `capture_coverage` emits for *repo*'s newest coverage
    artifact — `head_sha`/`branch`/`workflow_run_id` come straight off the
    artifact's own `workflow_run` object (`list_coverage_artifacts`'s
    docstring), not a second GitHub read. `source` names which of
    `_coverage_percent_from_json`'s two shapes produced `percent`.

    `branch` (`head_branch`) is GitHub-authored free text, the same
    untrusted-output contract `rows_from_ci_runs` enforces for its own
    `branch` (see that function's docstring): any collaborator who can push
    a branch controls `head_branch`. It goes through `SC.sanitise` here, at
    the one point this script turns the artifact's `workflow_run` object
    into its own row, so `emit_coverage`'s body string and `meta` dict only
    ever see the sanitised value. `head_sha` is not sanitised, for the
    reason `rows_from_ci_runs` gives: git constrains an object id to
    lowercase hex."""
    wr = artifact.get("workflow_run") or {}
    return {
        "repo": repo,
        "percent": percent,
        "source": source,
        "artifact_id": artifact.get("id"),
        "workflow_run_id": wr.get("id"),
        "head_sha": wr.get("head_sha") or "",
        "branch": SC.sanitise(wr.get("head_branch") or "", max_len=200),
        "artifact_created_at": artifact.get("created_at") or "",
    }


def capture_coverage(repo: str, token: str,
                     *, opener=urllib.request.urlopen) -> dict | None:
    """*repo*'s coverage row for this pass, or None when there is nothing to
    report this pass — no coverage artifact at all, no artifact qualifies
    (none rode *repo*'s default branch on a successful `ci.yml` run —
    `latest_qualifying_coverage_artifact`), or the one that does qualify is
    refused (oversized by its own metadata, oversized once actually read,
    not a valid zip, no usable JSON member, or its value matches neither of
    Orient's two shapes). None is the same `null` outcome Orient's own
    reader gives today for every one of those cases (CQ·636's body); it is
    not a failure and `main()` does not count it as one. Existing rows the
    Library already holds are untouched either way — this function only
    ever decides whether THIS pass has a new row to add.

    Raises `ArtifactsPermissionDenied` only from the LISTING read
    (`list_coverage_artifacts`). Raises `GA.Unreadable` from that same
    listing read, from `repo_default_branch`, or from a candidate's run
    check (`_run_is_successful_ci_run`) — every one a genuine inability to
    read GitHub, as opposed to a coverage artifact this script read but
    could not use or a candidate that simply does not qualify."""
    artifacts = list_coverage_artifacts(repo, token, opener=opener)
    if not artifacts:
        return None
    default_branch = repo_default_branch(repo, token, opener=opener)
    artifact = latest_qualifying_coverage_artifact(repo, artifacts, default_branch, token,
                                                   opener=opener)
    if artifact is None:
        print(f"pr-state-capture: {repo}: no coverage artifact from a successful ci.yml "
              f"run on {default_branch}; no coverage record this pass", file=sys.stderr)
        return None
    if not _artifact_within_cap(artifact, max_bytes=_COVERAGE_ARTIFACT_MAX_BYTES):
        print(f"pr-state-capture: {repo}: coverage artifact {artifact.get('id')} "
              f"reports size_in_bytes={artifact.get('size_in_bytes')!r}, over the "
              f"{_COVERAGE_ARTIFACT_MAX_BYTES}-byte cap; refused before downloading",
              file=sys.stderr)
        return None
    data = download_artifact_zip(repo, artifact["id"], token,
                                 max_bytes=_COVERAGE_DOWNLOAD_MAX_BYTES)
    if data is None:
        print(f"pr-state-capture: {repo}: coverage artifact {artifact['id']} read over "
              f"the {_COVERAGE_DOWNLOAD_MAX_BYTES}-byte cap; refused", file=sys.stderr)
        return None
    percent, source = parse_coverage_zip(data, member_max_bytes=_COVERAGE_MEMBER_MAX_BYTES)
    if percent is None:
        print(f"pr-state-capture: {repo}: coverage artifact {artifact['id']} carried no "
              f"usable coverage figure; no coverage record this pass", file=sys.stderr)
        return None
    return rows_from_coverage(repo, artifact, percent, source)


def coverage_dedup_key_for(row: dict) -> str:
    """The transition identity for a captured coverage figure: a repeated
    capture of the SAME (repo, artifact id) reuses this key — dedup is on
    the artifact id alone (this item's own spec: "post only when the
    artifact id changes"), not on the percentage, so a repeat read of the
    SAME artifact never re-posts even if a parse somehow gave a different
    number the second time."""
    return f"coverage:{row['repo']}:{row['artifact_id']}"


def _coverage_state_path(repo: str) -> Path:
    return STATE_DIR / f"{repo.replace('/', '__')}__coverage.json"


def load_delivered_coverage(repo: str) -> dict:
    """The last-delivered `{"artifact_id": ...}` for *repo*, or `{}` with one
    stderr line when nothing has been recorded yet or the record cannot be
    read — mirrors `load_delivered`/`load_delivered_ci_runs`'s self-heal, in
    its own file (`_coverage_state_path`) so a corrupt or missing coverage
    record never affects either sibling kind's delivery state or vice
    versa."""
    path = _coverage_state_path(repo)
    try:
        raw = path.read_text()
    except OSError:
        print(f"pr-state-capture: {repo}: no delivered coverage record yet; "
              f"this pass's coverage artifact (if any) will be posted", file=sys.stderr)
        return {}
    try:
        data = json.loads(raw)
    except ValueError:
        print(f"pr-state-capture: {repo}: delivered coverage record at {path} is "
              f"corrupt; this pass's coverage artifact (if any) will be posted",
              file=sys.stderr)
        return {}
    return data if isinstance(data, dict) else {}


def save_delivered_coverage(repo: str, delivered: dict) -> None:
    """Atomic write of *delivered* for *repo*'s coverage state — mirrors
    `save_delivered`/`save_delivered_ci_runs`'s atomic write, into the
    separate coverage state file."""
    STATE_DIR.mkdir(mode=0o700, parents=True, exist_ok=True)
    os.chmod(STATE_DIR, 0o700)
    path = _coverage_state_path(repo)
    tmp = path.with_name(f"{path.name}.{os.getpid()}.tmp")
    try:
        tmp.unlink()
    except OSError:
        pass
    fd = os.open(tmp, os.O_WRONLY | os.O_CREAT | os.O_EXCL, 0o600)
    with os.fdopen(fd, "w") as fh:
        fh.write(json.dumps(delivered, sort_keys=True))
    os.replace(tmp, path)


def dedup_key_for(row: dict) -> str:
    """The transition identity: a repeated capture of the SAME (repo, pr, head
    sha, state, draft flag, title) reuses this key, so the store's own dedup
    on `dedup_key` is what keeps it to one row — see the module docstring's
    section on the signal's own identity for why draft and title are in the
    key alongside head sha and state, and why the title component is
    `title_hash` (the title's identity form, hashed — see `_identity_title`)
    rather than the 200-char `title` field. `_delivered_identity` mirrors
    this same field set for the local send-state record, deliberately —
    anything that moves this key must move that record's identity too.

    `mergeable`/`review_decision` (CQ·636) are appended rather than
    `None`-omitted, so a PR whose only change is one of these two fields
    resolving still mints a distinct key from its prior row."""
    draft_flag = "draft" if row["draft"] else "ready"
    mergeable = row["mergeable"] or "none"
    review_decision = row["review_decision"] or "none"
    return (f"pr-state:{row['repo']}#{row['number']}:{row['head_sha']}:"
            f"{row['state']}:{draft_flag}:{row['title_hash']}:"
            f"{mergeable}:{review_decision}")


def emit(row: dict, *, tok: str | None = None, deadline: float | None = None,
        sleep=time.sleep) -> str:
    flag = " (draft)" if row["draft"] else ""
    body = (f"{row['repo']}#{row['number']} {row['state']}{flag}: "
            f"{row['title']} — {row['author']}")
    # The registry's project for the repo, the same mapping the Library applies
    # when it scopes a review record, so a PR's state and its reviews share one
    # product. product_for_channel maps Slack channels and filed most repos
    # under `platform`.
    product = projects.project_for_repo(row["repo"].split("/", 1)[-1])
    # produced_at is the PR's updated_at, not this pass's clock. The store keeps
    # only the first post of a dedup_key, so the stamp lands on the pass that
    # first sees a transition. GitHub's updated_at there falls between the
    # transition and the observation, so it is never later than this pass's clock
    # and equals the transition unless something else touched the PR in the
    # interval. updated_at is
    # deliberately NOT in dedup_key_for: a comment moves it without changing
    # anything the panel draws.
    #
    # `deadline`/`sleep` reach signal_client.post_signal's 429 handling: a 429
    # from the Library waits out its Retry-After and resends until this run's
    # own deadline passes (see _RUN_DEADLINE_SECONDS), at which point the
    # still-unsent signal comes back as 'error:429' here — main() treats that
    # specially (stops the rest of the pass rather than counting it as a
    # delivery failure like any other 'error:' result; see main()'s own 429
    # handling).
    return SC.post_structured(_KIND, _STREAM, body, product=product, meta=row,
                              dedup_key=dedup_key_for(row),
                              produced_at=row["updated_at"] or None,
                              deadline=deadline, sleep=sleep, tok=tok)


def emit_ci_run(row: dict, *, tok: str | None = None, deadline: float | None = None,
               sleep=time.sleep) -> str:
    concl = f"/{row['conclusion']}" if row["conclusion"] else ""
    body = (f"{row['repo']} ci.yml run {row['run_id']} on "
            f"{row['branch'] or '?'}: {row['status']}{concl}")
    product = projects.project_for_repo(row["repo"].split("/", 1)[-1])
    return SC.post_structured(_CI_RUN_KIND, _CI_RUN_STREAM, body, product=product, meta=row,
                              dedup_key=ci_run_dedup_key_for(row),
                              produced_at=row["updated_at"] or None,
                              deadline=deadline, sleep=sleep, tok=tok)


def emit_coverage(row: dict, *, tok: str | None = None, deadline: float | None = None,
                  sleep=time.sleep) -> str:
    body = (f"{row['repo']} coverage {row['percent']:.2f}% (artifact "
            f"{row['artifact_id']}, run {row['workflow_run_id']})")
    product = projects.project_for_repo(row["repo"].split("/", 1)[-1])
    return SC.post_structured(_COVERAGE_KIND, _COVERAGE_STREAM, body, product=product,
                              meta=row, dedup_key=coverage_dedup_key_for(row),
                              produced_at=row["artifact_created_at"] or None,
                              deadline=deadline, sleep=sleep, tok=tok)


def main() -> int:
    argparse.ArgumentParser(
        description="Capture per-repository pull-request state as signals, "
                    "authenticating as the install's own GitHub App.").parse_args()

    try:
        app = GA.installed_app()
    except GA.Unreadable as e:
        print(f"pr-state-capture: {e}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 1

    problem = GA.key_problem(app.key_path, label="the install's GitHub App")
    if problem:
        print(f"pr-state-capture: {problem}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 1
    try:
        jwt = GA.app_jwt(app.key_path, app.app_id)
    except GA.Unreadable as e:
        print(f"pr-state-capture: {e}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 1

    exit_code = 0
    sig_tok = signal_token()
    if not sig_tok:
        print(f"pr-state-capture: no {SIGNAL_TOKEN_FILE} and no ${SIGNAL_TOKEN_ENV}; "
              "sharing the agent-platform signal bucket until the Library mints "
              "pr-state-capture's own credential", file=sys.stderr)
    # One deadline for the whole run, not per signal: a 429 on signal 1 and a
    # 429 on signal 300 both resend against the same run budget, so a burst
    # early in the pass can't eat the time a later repo's signals need.
    deadline = time.monotonic() + _RUN_DEADLINE_SECONDS

    # Phase 1: read every repo, diff each row against what was last
    # delivered, and collect only the changed ones — see the module
    # docstring for why a pass no longer re-emits everything it reads. Repos
    # are grouped by their GitHub owner first (see the module docstring's
    # "ONE INSTALLATION PER OWNER" section): each owner's installation and
    # token are resolved on their own, so a failure resolving one owner
    # costs only that owner's repos. An owner the App is not installed on
    # (GA.NotInstalled, both lookups 404) is skipped with a notice and does
    # not set exit_code (see the module docstring's "AN OWNER THE APP IS NOT
    # INSTALLED ON IS SKIPPED, NOT FAILED" section); any other owner-level
    # failure is still a plain GA.Unreadable and still fails the pass.
    # The code-health repos (CQ·636), read once from the registry — a
    # SUBSET of repos_to_capture() (code_health_repos_for's own validation
    # refuses a code_health entry outside its unit's repos, and both
    # exclude private-project repos the same way), so every code-health
    # repo is also visited by the ordinary PR-state loop below under the
    # same owner.
    code_health_set = set(projects.code_health_repos())

    pending: list[dict] = []
    pending_ci: list[dict] = []
    pending_coverage: list[dict] = []
    delivered_by_repo: dict[str, dict] = {}
    delivered_ci_by_repo: dict[str, dict] = {}
    delivered_coverage_by_repo: dict[str, dict] = {}
    for owner, repos in sorted(_by_owner(repos_to_capture()).items()):
        try:
            inst = GA.installation_for_owner(jwt, owner, label="the install's GitHub App")
            inst_id = str(inst.get("id") or "")
            if not inst_id:
                raise GA.Unreadable("the installation carries no id")
            token = GA.installation_token(jwt, inst_id, permissions=_READ_ONLY_PERMISSIONS)
        except GA.NotInstalled:
            print(f"pr-state-capture: {owner}: the install's GitHub App is not "
                  f"installed on this owner; its {len(repos)} registered repos "
                  f"are skipped", file=sys.stderr)
            continue
        except GA.Unreadable as e:
            print(f"pr-state-capture: {owner}: {e}", file=sys.stderr)
            exit_code = 1
            continue

        # A repo absent from the installation's list is skipped, not failed, ONLY
        # when the installation covers every repository this owner has: absence
        # then means the repo is not on GitHub. Under a "selected" installation
        # absence also means "the App was never granted it", which must stay loud,
        # so every repo is read and a 404 fails the pass as before.
        present: set[str] | None = None
        if inst.get("repository_selection") == "all":
            try:
                present = installed_repos(token)
            except GA.Unreadable as e:
                print(f"pr-state-capture: {e}; reading every registered repo instead",
                      file=sys.stderr)
                exit_code = 1

        # A SEPARATE token for the code-health phase (CQ·636), minted only
        # when this owner actually has a code-health repo — see
        # `_CODE_HEALTH_PERMISSIONS`'s comment for why this is not folded
        # into the token above. A refusal here (the installation does not
        # grant `actions: read`) costs only this owner's code-health
        # capture, logged, not fatal to this owner's PR-state capture
        # (which already has its own token) and not fatal to the pass.
        ch_token = None
        owner_code_health_repos = [r for r in repos if r in code_health_set]
        if owner_code_health_repos:
            try:
                ch_token = GA.installation_token(jwt, inst_id,
                                                 permissions=_CODE_HEALTH_PERMISSIONS)
            except GA.Unreadable as e:
                print(f"pr-state-capture: {owner}: code-health capture skipped "
                      f"for {len(owner_code_health_repos)} repo(s): {e}", file=sys.stderr)

        for repo in repos:
            if present is not None and repo.lower() not in present:
                print(f"pr-state-capture: {repo}: not on GitHub (the installation covers "
                      f"every repository and does not list it); skipped", file=sys.stderr)
                continue
            try:
                pulls = list_pulls(repo, token)
            except GA.Unreadable as e:
                print(f"pr-state-capture: {e}", file=sys.stderr)
                exit_code = 1
                continue
            rows = rows_from_pulls(repo, pulls)
            # Open-PR mergeable/review_decision (CQ·636): one GraphQL call,
            # only when this repo has an open PR, reusing the SAME
            # read-only token every other read in this loop already holds
            # (no new permission needed for this one).
            if any(row["state"] == "OPEN" for row in rows):
                try:
                    decisions = fetch_open_pr_decisions(repo, token)
                except GA.Unreadable as e:
                    print(f"pr-state-capture: {e}", file=sys.stderr)
                    exit_code = 1
                    decisions = {}
                for row in rows:
                    d = decisions.get(row["number"])
                    if d:
                        row["mergeable"] = d["mergeable"]
                        row["review_decision"] = d["review_decision"]
            delivered = load_delivered(repo)
            delivered_by_repo[repo] = delivered
            pending.extend(_changed_rows(rows, delivered))

            if ch_token is not None and repo in owner_code_health_repos:
                try:
                    runs = (list_ci_runs(repo, ch_token)
                           + list_ci_runs(repo, ch_token, branch="main"))
                except GA.Unreadable as e:
                    print(f"pr-state-capture: {e}", file=sys.stderr)
                    exit_code = 1
                else:
                    ci_delivered = load_delivered_ci_runs(repo)
                    delivered_ci_by_repo[repo] = ci_delivered
                    pending_ci.extend(_changed_ci_runs(
                        rows_from_ci_runs(repo, runs), ci_delivered))

                # Coverage (CQ·636's fourth item), the SAME `ch_token` —
                # `actions: read` already covers artifacts, not only
                # workflow runs. A permission gap confirmed by name
                # (403, `ArtifactsPermissionDenied`) is logged and skips
                # only this repo's coverage; any other read failure is a
                # plain `GA.Unreadable` and still fails the pass, exactly
                # like the CI-run read above. `capture_coverage` returning
                # None (no artifact, or one refused/unusable) is neither —
                # it is this pass's `null`, not a failure.
                try:
                    cov_row = capture_coverage(repo, ch_token)
                except ArtifactsPermissionDenied as e:
                    print(f"pr-state-capture: {e}; no coverage capture for "
                          f"this repo this pass", file=sys.stderr)
                except GA.Unreadable as e:
                    print(f"pr-state-capture: {e}", file=sys.stderr)
                    exit_code = 1
                else:
                    if cov_row is not None:
                        cov_delivered = load_delivered_coverage(repo)
                        delivered_coverage_by_repo[repo] = cov_delivered
                        if cov_delivered.get("artifact_id") != cov_row["artifact_id"]:
                            pending_coverage.append(cov_row)

    # Phase 2: newest-updated first, across every repo together — not
    # repo-by-repo — so a fresh merge in one repo is never queued behind an
    # older repo's backlog.
    pending.sort(key=lambda row: row["updated_at"], reverse=True)
    pending_ci.sort(key=lambda row: row["updated_at"], reverse=True)
    pending_coverage.sort(key=lambda row: row["artifact_created_at"], reverse=True)

    # Phase 3: emit, stopping the WHOLE pass — every remaining row in every
    # repo, not just the one being posted — the first time a 429 survives
    # signal_client.post_signal's own retry/backoff against `deadline`. The
    # Library's limit is per submitter, so once it has been hit, trying the
    # next row costs the same 429 for no gain; everything from that point on
    # is left pending for the next pass instead, and reported as one summary
    # line rather than once per row (see the module docstring's incident:
    # one line per refused PR is what grew the launchd log to 6 MB).
    sent = 0
    rate_limited = False
    touched: set[str] = set()
    for row in pending:
        if rate_limited:
            continue
        buf = io.StringIO()
        with contextlib.redirect_stderr(buf):
            result = emit(row, tok=sig_tok, deadline=deadline)
        if result == "error:429":
            rate_limited = True
            continue
        captured = buf.getvalue()
        if captured:
            sys.stderr.write(captured)
        if result.startswith("error"):
            print(f"pr-state-capture: {row['repo']}#{row['number']} NOT "
                  f"delivered: {result}", file=sys.stderr)
            exit_code = 1
            continue
        if result in ("created", "duplicate"):
            sent += 1
            delivered_by_repo[row["repo"]][str(row["number"])] = _delivered_identity(row)
            touched.add(row["repo"])
        # 'skip' means no token at all: nothing landed and nothing to record,
        # same as before this change — not itself a pass failure.

    for repo in touched:
        save_delivered(repo, delivered_by_repo[repo])

    # Same stop-on-429 contract as the PR-row loop above, sharing the one
    # `rate_limited` flag: a 429 hit while emitting PR rows means the
    # Library's per-submitter bucket is already spent for this pass, so the
    # CI-run loop below no-ops for the rest of this pass too rather than
    # spending the same 429 again for no gain.
    sent_ci = 0
    touched_ci: set[str] = set()
    for row in pending_ci:
        if rate_limited:
            continue
        buf = io.StringIO()
        with contextlib.redirect_stderr(buf):
            result = emit_ci_run(row, tok=sig_tok, deadline=deadline)
        if result == "error:429":
            rate_limited = True
            continue
        captured = buf.getvalue()
        if captured:
            sys.stderr.write(captured)
        if result.startswith("error"):
            print(f"pr-state-capture: {row['repo']} ci.yml run {row['run_id']} "
                  f"NOT delivered: {result}", file=sys.stderr)
            exit_code = 1
            continue
        if result in ("created", "duplicate"):
            sent_ci += 1
            delivered_ci_by_repo[row["repo"]][str(row["run_id"])] = _ci_run_identity(row)
            touched_ci.add(row["repo"])

    for repo in touched_ci:
        save_delivered_ci_runs(repo, delivered_ci_by_repo[repo])

    # Same stop-on-429 contract, sharing the one `rate_limited` flag — a 429
    # hit while emitting PR rows or CI runs means the Library's per-submitter
    # bucket is already spent for this pass, so this loop no-ops for the
    # rest of this pass too.
    sent_coverage = 0
    touched_coverage: set[str] = set()
    for row in pending_coverage:
        if rate_limited:
            continue
        buf = io.StringIO()
        with contextlib.redirect_stderr(buf):
            result = emit_coverage(row, tok=sig_tok, deadline=deadline)
        if result == "error:429":
            rate_limited = True
            continue
        captured = buf.getvalue()
        if captured:
            sys.stderr.write(captured)
        if result.startswith("error"):
            print(f"pr-state-capture: {row['repo']} coverage artifact "
                  f"{row['artifact_id']} NOT delivered: {result}", file=sys.stderr)
            exit_code = 1
            continue
        if result in ("created", "duplicate"):
            sent_coverage += 1
            delivered_coverage_by_repo[row["repo"]] = {"artifact_id": row["artifact_id"]}
            touched_coverage.add(row["repo"])

    for repo in touched_coverage:
        save_delivered_coverage(repo, delivered_coverage_by_repo[repo])

    if rate_limited:
        print(f"pr-state-capture: stopped on the Library's rate limit: sent "
              f"{sent} PR row(s), {sent_ci} CI run(s) and {sent_coverage} "
              f"coverage record(s); {len(pending) - sent} PR row(s), "
              f"{len(pending_ci) - sent_ci} CI run(s) and "
              f"{len(pending_coverage) - sent_coverage} coverage record(s) left "
              f"pending for the next pass", file=sys.stderr)

    return exit_code


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main())
